            <ArticleTitle>[Physical activity, obesity and self-esteem in chilean schoolchildren].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>299-308</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0034-98872017000300006</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4067/S0034-98872017000300006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Obesity is a worldwide epidemic disease and a problem for the Chilean society.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To analyze the relationship between physical condition, body mass index (BMI), level of physical activity and self-esteem. Material ad Methods: Questionnaires to assess self-esteem (Rosemberg scale) and levels of physical activity (Physical Activity Questionnaire for older Children, PAQ-C) were answered by 515 children aged 10.5 ± 0.5 years from 27 schools of Santiago de Chile. BMI was calculated. Course-Navette test was carried out, vertical jump and hand dynamometry were measured. For statistical analysis, structural equations were used.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">An acceptable goodness of fit for the models was found. There was a positive relationship between BMI and hand dynamometry, as well as a negative relationship between BMI and maximal oxygen consumption, jumping ability, physical activity and self-esteem. Finally, self-esteem was positively related to physical activity engagement.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In these children, self-esteem was related to physical activity variables.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
